module Core

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   A rendering placed in a column - the other half of `printf`'s width, and the half that is not
   about numbers at all.
   ==========================================================================================

   `%08x` conflates two operations: writing a number with at least eight digits, and placing a
   rendering in a column eight units wide. `Format/Number.yana`'s `digits` is the first and this is
   the second, and keeping them apart is what lets each one be simple - `printf`'s zero flag has to
   be defined against its sign, against its precision and against left-justification, because it is
   trying to be both at once.

   **This works on anything that can be shown**, which is the whole reason it is a formatter of its
   own: `padded(name, 12)`, `padded(hex(x, digits: 8), 12)` and `padded(fixed(cost), 10)` are the
   same operation over three different renderings, and none of the three had to know about it.

   **A column is counted in code units.** That is what a monospaced terminal aligns and what every
   other function in this library counts, so it is the number a caller can predict. Text whose units
   are not its glyphs - a combining sequence, an East Asian width, a `\t` - is text this cannot align
   and neither can `printf`; what it promises is a count, not a typesetter.
-}

{-
   Where the fill goes, which is the same question as which edge the value sits against.

   `Leading` is the default because it is `printf`'s: `%12s` puts the fill in front, and a column of
   numbers is the case that has to line up. `Trailing` is what a column of names wants, and
   `Balanced` is a heading over either - split evenly, with the odd unit going after the value, so a
   caller who centers two strings of different parity does not get them off by a unit in opposite
   directions.
-}
pub data Padding
  = @value(0) Leading
  | @value(1) Trailing
  | @value(2) Balanced

{-
   A rendering and the column it goes in.

   The subject is `'a` like every other formatter here, so `padded(hex(n), 10)` refers to the
   `NumberFormat` rather than copying it and the `NumberFormat` refers to `n`. Two borrows deep, and
   the return-root check bounds both: neither may leave the expression that built it.

   `fill` is a `CodeUnit` rather than a `Maybe` for the reason `NumberFormat`'s `group` is one, and
   here the default is a real space rather than a sentinel - a column padded with nothing is a column
   that does not exist, so there is no "no fill" case to spell.

   `width` is a *minimum*. A rendering wider than the column is written whole and pushes the row out,
   which is the only answer that does not lose data; a caller who wants it clipped is asking for a
   different operation and should say so where the text is made.
-}
pub data Aligned(a) {
    value: 'a,
    width: Size,
    padding: Padding = Leading,
    fill: CodeUnit = 32
}

-- The constructor, which is what a call site writes. `padded(x, 12)` right-aligns in twelve units;
-- the two other placements and a different fill unit are named arguments, since a call site reading
-- `padded(name, 12, Trailing)` says less than `padded(name, 12, padding: Trailing)` does.
pub fn (Show(a)) padded(value: 'a, width: Size, padding: Padding = Leading, fill: CodeUnit = 32) -> Aligned(a) =
    Aligned {value: value, width: width, padding: padding, fill: fill}

-- `count` fill units, and nothing at all for a count that is not positive - which is the ordinary
-- case of a rendering at or over its column's width.
fn pushFill(&to: String, fill: CodeUnit, count: Size):
    let &left = count

    while left > 0:
        String.pushUnit(to, fill :: Int)
        left = left - 1

{-
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Writing the column
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   **`Trailing` writes straight into the destination and the other two cannot**, which is the one
   thing about this instance worth reading. Fill that goes *after* the value is fill appended once
   the value's own units are counted, so the value is written where it belongs and the position it
   started at answers how wide it was. Fill that goes *before* it needs that width first, and the
   only ways to have it are to render twice or to render somewhere else - so those two render into a
   scratch `String` sized by the value's own `showBound` and append it after the fill.

   The scratch is one allocation on a native target and a host string on JS, and it is spent only by
   the placements that need it. A `Trailing` column costs exactly what the value costs plus its fill.
-}
instance (Show(a)) Show(Aligned(a)):
    fn show(value: Aligned(a), &to: String):
        if value.padding is Trailing:
            let start = length(to)
            show(value.value, to)
            pushFill(to, value.fill, value.width - (length(to) - start))
            return

        let &rendered = String.ofCapacity(formatBound(showBound(value.value)))
        show(value.value, rendered)

        let short = value.width - length(rendered)
        let before = if value.padding is Balanced then short / 2 else short

        pushFill(to, value.fill, before)
        String.push(to, rendered)
        pushFill(to, value.fill, short - before)

    {-
       The column's width or the value's own bound, whichever is larger - and `Nothing` when the
       value has none.

       A bound may be generous and may not be short, so a value that cannot say how wide it gets
       makes the whole column unbounded: answering `width` there would promise a size the very next
       long string breaks. `formatBound` turns that into a seed of zero and the appends grow past it,
       which is Implementation-Storage.md part 8's strategy (c) reached the way every other
       unbounded hole reaches it.
    -}
    fn showBound(value: Aligned(a)) -> Maybe(Size) = match showBound(value.value):
        Just(units) -> Just(if units > value.width then units else value.width)
        Nothing -> Nothing
